Tests for the us3_sql scripts
=============================

catalog_smoke.sh      smoke test for the procedures in us3_catalog_procs.sql
catalog_fixture.sql   the data set it loads
catalog_mutations.sh  checks that catalog_smoke.sh catches broken procedures
write_procs_test.sh   live test of delete_editedData and of the procedures
                      that report a new record's ID (create_buffer_component,
                      add_buffer_component)

All of them run against a real server: they create a scratch database, load
us3.sql, us3_procedures.sql and catalog_fixture.sql into it, and drop it
again at the end, also when they fail or are interrupted (KEEP_DB=1 leaves
it behind for a closer look).


Running them
------------

Give the client command line in MYSQL, without a database name.  Keep the
password off it: put the connection settings in an option file,

    [client]
    host     = 127.0.0.1
    port     = 3306
    user     = root
    password = secret

and pass that with --defaults-extra-file (it must be the first option):

    MYSQL="mysql --defaults-extra-file=$HOME/smoke.cnf" tests/catalog_smoke.sh

or export the password as MYSQL_PWD.  A -psecret on the command line also
works -- the client's warnings go to a file, not into what the tests
parse, and a call counts as failed only by the client's exit status -- but
it puts the password in the process list.  The MariaDB client is used the
same way (MYSQL="mariadb --defaults-extra-file=...").

Each prints "== N passed, 0 failed" and exits 0 when everything passes,
1 when a check fails and 2 when the database could not be set up.  The
scratch database is us3_catalog_smoke (SMOKE_DB) for the smoke test and
the mutations, us3_write_procs_test (WRITE_DB) for write_procs_test.sh;
it is DROPPED first.

The account needs to create databases and routines.  On a server with
binary logging, which MySQL 8 has on by default, creating the functions in
us3_procedures.sql also needs SUPER, or the server started with
log_bin_trust_function_creators = 1; otherwise they fail with error 1418.


catalog_smoke.sh
----------------

Calls each catalog procedure as an administrator (for everybody and for
one investigator), as each investigator, as a stranger, and with every
kind of failed login: a bad password, an account that does not exist, one
not activated, one disabled, one at userlevel 1, and an empty personGUID.
For each call it checks:

  - the status;
  - the names and order of the data set's columns, against golden lists
    taken from the row readers in UltraScan3's utils/us_data_catalog.cpp
    (which read the columns by position) -- also for
    get_model_desc_by_editID and get_noise_desc_by_editID, which the same
    readers read;
  - the number of rows, and for many calls which records, in which order;
  - the values of the columns callers use: IDs, GUIDs, file names,
    checksums and sizes (and that the *_by_person procedures hash
    nothing: their checksum and size are NULL), and that each UTC_ column
    holds what timestamp2UTC() gives for the fixture's timestamp and is a
    DATETIME, not the table's TIMESTAMP (on a server running in UTC a
    dropped conversion would not change the value);
  - that every count get_experiment_summary reports is what the matching
    detail procedure returns for the same caller.

The fixture covers the cases that are easy to get wrong: an experiment
with three experimentPerson rows (two of them the same person), a model
another investigator fitted to someone's edit, a model and noise record
with no modelPerson row, an empty experiment, one linked to nobody,
experiment 1 (which verify_experiment_permission() opens to anyone, and
the catalog procedures must not), global-fit noise recorded against one
experiment's edit but fitted with another experiment's model, and a model
on the UNASSIGNED edit.  Its record IDs are not in the order the
procedures sort by, and its timestamps are fixed.

SMOKE_CATALOG=file loads that file after us3_procedures.sql, over the
catalog procedures it sources.


catalog_mutations.sh
--------------------

Proves the smoke test can fail.  For each of its mutations -- two columns
swapped, a UTC conversion dropped, an edit checksum that is NULL, a
*_by_person procedure that hashes its blobs, an ORDER BY removed, noise
counted or listed by its edit rather than its model, experiment 1 open to
anyone -- it loads a broken copy of us3_catalog_procs.sql
(SMOKE_CATALOG) and expects catalog_smoke.sh to fail.  It prints "caught"
or "SURVIVED" for each, and exits 1 if any survived, 2 if a mutation no
longer applies to the file.  Name mutations as arguments to run only
those.  The whole set takes two minutes or so.


write_procs_test.sh
-------------------

delete_editedData: deletes the edit, its models and all their noise, and
noise recorded against the edit for another edit's model; is all or
nothing when the edit cannot be deleted (a stand-in table refers to it),
both on its own and inside a caller's transaction, which it must neither
commit nor lose; refuses the UNASSIGNED edit 1 and strangers.

create_buffer_component: takes a free ID when the table has a gap, and
reports that ID through last_insertID() rather than the connection's
previous insert; reports 0 when refused.  add_buffer_component reports 0.


us3.sql on MySQL 8.0
--------------------

us3.sql does not load cleanly on MySQL 8.0 (checked on 8.0.46).  The
tests therefore load it with --force, print whatever failed (the smoke
test does), and only insist on the tables the procedures read.  What
fails today:

  - line 7: SET SQL_MODE names NO_AUTO_CREATE_USER, which MySQL 8.0
    removed (error 1231).  The statement fails, so the session keeps the
    server's default sql_mode, which has NO_ZERO_DATE, and so:
  - HPCAnalysisRequest and HPCAnalysisResult are not created: their
    zero-date TIMESTAMP defaults are invalid under NO_ZERO_DATE (1067).
    They load once NO_AUTO_CREATE_USER is dropped from line 7.
  - autoflowAnalysis, autoflowAnalysisHistory, autoflowStages,
    autoflowAnalysisStages, autoflowIntensity, autoflowAnalysisABDEStages
    and autoflowAnalysisVelMwlStages are not created: they give TEXT
    columns literal defaults (DEFAULT "localhost" and the like), which
    MySQL 8.0 refuses in any sql_mode (1101).  MariaDB accepts them.
  - the four DROP INDEX IF EXISTS / CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS statements
    for personal_access_tokens are MariaDB syntax (1064); the table itself
    is created without those two indexes.

None of these tables is used by the procedures tested here, and
us3_procedures.sql loads with no errors.  One more MySQL 8.0 difference
shows only when procedures run: get_instrument_info_new,
add_instrument_new, update_instrument_new and decode_instrument_passw
(us3_hardware_procs.sql) call ENCODE() / DECODE(), which MySQL 8.0.3
removed, so they fail with "FUNCTION ... DECODE does not exist".

On MariaDB (checked on 10.11.14) us3.sql loads with no errors at all, and
the tests pass there as well as on MySQL 8.0.46.
